    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic post-traumatic back and pelvic pain with right leg neuropathic features (G89.21)</content>. PEG 7.0, slightly better than 7.3 at intake. Duloxetine 30 mg is tolerated after two weeks. [S]<list><item>Increase duloxetine to 60 mg daily.</item><item>Physical therapy twice weekly from today, with daily home exercises.</item><item>Referral for cognitive behavioral therapy for chronic pain.</item><item>Continue acetaminophen 1 g three times a day, no more than 3 g a day.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the right ankle and foot (M19.171)</content>. Ankle pain limits standing; no effusion. The diclofenac gel helps. [S]<list><item>Continue diclofenac gel four times daily.</item><item>Ankle radiograph not repeated.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Right leg weakness and shortening after childhood polio (B91)</content>. Leg-length difference adds to back and ankle strain. [S]<list><item>Shoe lift prescribed by the rehabilitation team.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Long-term opioid therapy, 60 MME a day (Z79.891)</content>. No sedation, constipation, or falls. Single prescriber; naloxone at home. [S]<list><item>No change in the oxycodone dose today.</item><item>First reduction of about 10 percent at the four-week visit, as she agreed; it can be paused if pain or function worsens.</item><item>Keep the oxycodone in a locked box.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency (E55.9)</content>. On cholecalciferol 2,000 units daily for two weeks. [S]<list><item>Recheck 25-hydroxyvitamin D about three months after starting.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PEG pain, enjoyment of life, and general activity score</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7.0, from 7.3 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 depression score, with item 9</td><td>Every 2 to 4 weeks during active treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>8 at intake with item 9 scored 0; next at the four-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Opioid benefits and harms: function, sedation, constipation, falls</td><td>Every visit during a taper; at least every 3 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No sedation, constipation, or falls; function slowly improving</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Prescription drug monitoring program check</td><td>Before each opioid prescription; at least every 3 months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Checked at intake: a single prescriber and no sedatives. No opioid prescription today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine drug test, with consent</td><td>Before long-term therapy, then at least annually</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake, consistent with the prescribed oxycodone only</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home, with a family member trained</td><td>Offer when starting or continuing opioids; review each visit</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Dispensed at intake, with her spouse taught to use it</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Concurrent benzodiazepines or other sedatives</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None prescribed or taken</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Duloxetine tolerance after starting</td><td>About 2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Brief nausea only; blood pressure 116/72. Increased to 60 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>ALT on regular acetaminophen</td><td>Periodically when acetaminophen approaches 3 g a day</td><td>Up to date</td><td>22 U/L at intake; recheck at the six-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>CBC, creatinine, and blood pressure for chronic oral NSAID use</td><td>Every 6 to 12 months on oral NSAIDs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Topical diclofenac only; creatinine 0.72 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Right ankle examination: range of motion, effusion, alignment</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Bony enlargement and reduced motion; no effusion</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Right ankle radiograph</td><td>Only for diagnostic uncertainty, clinical change, or surgical planning</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Done at intake; no routine surveillance imaging</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Physical medicine and rehabilitation</td><td>Once, for a multimodal rehabilitation plan</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; physical therapy and a shoe lift</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for chronic pain</td><td>Early in a multimodal plan</td><td>Referred</td><td>Referred today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise program adherence</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Physical therapy twice weekly starts today, with home exercises</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>About 3 months after starting repletion</td><td>Due</td><td>18 ng/mL at intake; recheck in about eleven weeks</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Improve the PEG score by at least 30% from baseline. In progress; target 2026-10-29 [S]</item><item>Stand at the easel for 30 minutes at a time by the six-week review. In progress; target 2026-10-29 [S]</item><item>First opioid dose reduction, agreed by the patient, without loss of function. In progress; target 2026-10-29 [S]</item><item>Naloxone in the home with a family member trained to use it. Outcome by 2026-09-17: Naloxone nasal spray dispensed; spouse taught at the first visit [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain — United States, 2022 (2022); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Low Back Pain (2022); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, osteoarthritis and depression monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Physical therapy twice weekly. Four-week chronic pain follow-up in two weeks for the first oxycodone reduction. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-15: four-week chronic pain follow-up with the first opioid dose reduction. [S]</paragraph></text>

